Experment 1.1 Contains duplicate (Leetcode Problem-217)
Aim: Given an integer array nums, return true if any value appears at least twice in the array, and return false if every element is distinct.

Algorithm: 
1. Sort the array in ascending order.
2. Traverse the array from the second element (i = 1).
3. Compare each element with its previous element.
4. If two adjacent elements are equal, return true (duplicate found).
5. If the loop finishes without finding any equal adjacent elements, return false.

Code: 
class Solution {
public:
    bool containsDuplicate(vector<int>& nums) {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        for(int i=1; i<nums.size(); i++)
        {
            if(nums[i]==nums[i-1])
            {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
};


Experment 1.2 Contains duplicate II (Leetcode Problem-219)
Aim: Given an integer array nums and an integer k, return true if there are two distinct indices i and j in the array such that nums[i] == nums[j] and abs(i - j) <= k.

Algorithm:
1. Create an unordered map to store each element and its latest index.
2. Traverse the array from left to right.
4. For each element:
     If it already exists in the map, calculate the index difference:
     abs(i - previousIndex).
     If the difference is ≤ k, return true.
5. Update the map with the current index of the element.
6. If no such pair is found after traversing the array, return false.

Code: 
class Solution {
public:
    bool containsNearbyDuplicate(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        unordered_map<int,int>mp;
        for (int i=0; i<nums.size(); i++)
        {
            if (mp.find(nums[i])!=mp.end() && abs (i-mp[nums[i]]) <=k)
            {
                return true;
            }
            mp[nums[i]]=i;
        }
        return false;
    }
};
